  values ('ME', 'long_answer', 'Scholars consider Bhutan''s democracy to be unique and term it a ''home-grown natural democracy''. Substantiate with appropriate reasons why Bhutan''s democracy is considered unique and a home-grown natural democracy.
Give your answer by reflecting on how Bhutanese democracy has evolved and been nurtured in keeping with the visions of our Monarchs. (20 marks)', 'Bhutan''s democracy is called unique and home-grown because it was not won through struggle, revolution or foreign pressure; it was given by the Throne to the people, often against their initial wishes, and was carefully designed to suit Bhutan''s culture and values.
Evolution under the visions of our Monarchs:
1. The Third Druk Gyalpo, Jigme Dorji Wangchuck, established the National Assembly (Tshogdu) in 1953, abolished serfdom, set up the Royal Advisory Council (1965), the Council of Ministers (1968) and the High Court, and gave the Assembly power to pass laws.
2. The Fourth Druk Gyalpo, Jigme Singye Wangchuck, decentralised power through the Dzongkhag Yargay Tshogchung (1981) and Gewog Yargay Tshogchung (1991); in 1998 he devolved full executive powers to an elected Council of Ministers and introduced a provision for the National Assembly to remove the King by a vote of no-confidence; in 2001 he commanded the drafting of the Constitution and personally consulted the people in every dzongkhag (2005); and in 2006 he abdicated in favour of the Crown Prince to ensure a smooth transition.
3. The Fifth Druk Gyalpo, Jigme Khesar Namgyel Wangchuck, oversaw mock elections (2007), the first National Council (2007-08) and National Assembly (2008) elections, and signed the Constitution on 18 July 2008; he continues to guide democracy, reminding citizens of their duties.
Home-grown features: the Constitution is rooted in Buddhist values and Gross National Happiness; it keeps the Monarchy as head of state and guardian of the Constitution, with the King retiring at 65 and a procedure for abdication; it has an apolitical National Council; a primary round that leads to a two-party contest, ensuring stable government and a strong opposition; minimum educational qualifications for candidates; and constitutional duties to preserve culture and the environment (60% forest cover).
Thus Bhutan''s democracy grew gradually, from the top down, in harmony with tradition - a natural evolution guided by visionary Monarchs.', null, '[{"point": "Unique: democracy granted from the Throne, not through revolution, demand or external pressure; people initially reluctant", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Evolution under the Monarchs: 3rd King - Tshogdu (National Assembly, 1953), Royal Advisory Council (1965), Council of Ministers (1968), High Court; 4th King - DYT (1981), GYT (1991), devolution to elected cabinet and vote of confidence on King (1998), Constitution drafting (2001), public consultations (2005), abdication (2006); 5th King - first elections 2007-08, Constitution adopted 18 July 2008, continued guidance", "marks": 10}, {"point": "Home-grown features: rooted in Buddhist values, GNH, culture; apolitical National Council; two-party system through primary round; King''s retirement at 65 and abdication provision; 60% forest cover; strong monarchy as guardian", "marks": 6}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'democracy-in-bhutan'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 20,
