  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'In pursuit of Bhutan''s ''High Value, Low Volume'' tourism policy, and to differentiate itself from other tourist destinations, Bhutan raised its Sustainable Development Fee to USD 200 per person per night with effect from 1 July 2022.
What do you think of this decision? Assess the government''s rationale behind this policy change. Analyse the impact of this policy on Bhutan''s tourism industry and the economy as a whole. (20 marks)', 'My view: the decision was bold and consistent with Bhutan''s ''high value, low volume'' philosophy, but its sudden implementation after COVID caused hardship, and later adjustments showed the need for balance.
Rationale: (1) to protect Bhutan''s environment, culture and sacred sites from the pressures of mass tourism; (2) to attract visitors who value Bhutan''s uniqueness and spend more, rather than large numbers of budget tourists; (3) the fee funds carbon-offsetting, conservation, free health care and education, and infrastructure - tourists contribute to sustainable development; (4) to raise service standards across hotels, guides and operators as part of the tourism reform; (5) regional tourists, who had grown rapidly and paid no SDF, were brought into the system; (6) to rebrand Bhutan as an exclusive destination after reopening.
Impact on the tourism industry: arrivals fell sharply after reopening in September 2022; hotels, tour operators, guides, drivers, restaurants and handicraft sellers - many already hurt by COVID - lost income, and many workers left the sector or the country; businesses were pushed to improve quality; Bhutan became less competitive with cheaper Himalayan destinations. The government responded by halving the fee to USD 100 from September 2023 (until 2027) and offering incentives such as longer stays for the same fee.
Impact on the economy: in the short term, lower tourism receipts reduced foreign exchange earnings and revenue, and increased unemployment; in the long term, higher revenue per visitor and SDF funds can support sustainable development and protect the assets that tourism depends on. Overall, the policy fits Bhutan''s values, but it must be balanced with the livelihoods of people working in tourism.', null, '[{"point": "Own view with reasons", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Rationale: high value low volume, protect environment and culture from mass tourism, offset carbon footprint and fund conservation and development, attract high-spending visitors, raise service standards, reset after COVID, brand Bhutan; regional tourists also pay", "marks": 7}, {"point": "Impact on tourism industry: sharp fall in arrivals initially, hotels/ guides/ drivers/ handicrafts lose income and jobs, push for quality upgrading, competition with other destinations; later adjustments (USD 100 from Sept 2023, incentives)", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Impact on economy: lower foreign exchange and revenue initially, unemployment and emigration of tourism workers, but higher revenue per tourist and funds for SDF projects; long-term sustainability", "marks": 5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'tourism-policy'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 20,
    2160, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
