  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'Bhutan constructed its first-ever pedestrian terminal in Phuentsholing in September 2022, with the objective of putting in place a facility to document and regulate the entry and exit of all Bhutanese and foreigners alike.
What do you think of this initiative? Justify your opinion. What impact does this have on Bhutan in terms of the economy, its foreign policy and diplomatic relations with its neighbouring nation, and national security? (20 marks)', 'Opinion: I think the pedestrian terminal is a positive and necessary initiative. Phuentsholing is the busiest entry point, and for decades people crossed between Phuentsholing and Jaigaon without records. With the border reopening after COVID-19 in September 2022, a modern terminal allows orderly, documented entry and exit for everyone, as most countries do.
Economy: in the short term, documentation and permit requirements reduced casual cross-border movement, affecting shops and businesses on both sides and causing some inconvenience to daily commuters and workers; but in the long term a regulated border supports formal trade, collects fees such as the SDF from tourists, manages foreign workers properly and gives data for economic planning. Efficient processes (digital permits, multiple lanes) are needed to keep trade and tourism flowing.
Foreign policy and relations with India: Bhutan and India share an open border and a special friendship under the 2007 Friendship Treaty. Since Indian nationals also pass through the terminal, the initiative must be implemented with close consultation and coordination with India, explained as modern border management rather than restriction, and with facilitation for genuine visitors, traders and workers so that goodwill is maintained.
National security: the terminal helps control illegal immigration, track people entering and leaving, prevent crime and the smuggling of drugs and contraband, screen for diseases in future pandemics, and support counter-terrorism efforts - all vital for a small country with a porous border.', null, '[{"point": "Clear opinion with justification: positive - orderly, documented movement, modern border management, reopening after COVID", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Economy: some inconvenience and initial decline in cross-border shopping and trade for Phuentsholing/ Jaigaon businesses, but controlled flow of workers and tourists, revenue (SDF/ permits), formalised trade, data for planning", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Foreign policy and relations with India: applies to all including Indian nationals - needs close coordination and goodwill with India; open border tradition under 1949/ 2007 treaty; cooperation rather than restriction; careful communication", "marks": 5}, {"point": "National security: control illegal immigration, crime, smuggling of drugs and goods, health screening (pandemics), records of who enters and exits, counter terrorism", "marks": 6}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'border-management-and-foreign-relations'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 20,
    2160, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (General): BCSE 2023, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2023 Main Examination, General category, Paper III", "year": 2023, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, null, null, null, null, null, null)
