  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'Bhutan will graduate from the Least Developed Countries (LDC) category in December 2023.
What does this mean for Bhutan? How will this affect the achievement of Bhutan''s vision of a ''Developed Bhutan''? With the 12th Five Year Plan ending and the 13th Five Year Plan in preparation, what strategies should Bhutan adopt to ensure a smooth and sustainable transition after graduation from the LDC category and achieve the ''Developed Bhutan'' vision? (20 marks)', 'Meaning: graduating from the LDC category (on 13 December 2023) means that Bhutan has met the United Nations criteria - income per capita and human assets (health and education) - for leaving the group of the world''s poorest countries. It is international recognition of decades of development under the Monarchs and successive plans. But it also means gradually losing LDC-specific support: grants and highly concessional loans, special trade preferences (duty-free, quota-free access in some markets), technical assistance and dedicated funds.
Effect on the ''Developed Bhutan'' vision: graduation boosts confidence and Bhutan''s image with investors and partners, and is a step towards becoming a developed, high-income country. However, Bhutan remains economically vulnerable - small, landlocked, dependent on hydropower and imports, and exposed to climate disasters - so losing concessional finance and trade preferences, and paying higher borrowing costs, could slow progress if not managed well.
Strategies for a smooth, sustainable transition:
1. Implement a smooth transition strategy with development partners to phase out support gradually and secure continued preferences (e.g. GSP+ with the EU, bilateral agreements).
2. Diversify the economy beyond hydropower - tourism, agriculture and agro-processing, manufacturing, ICT and digital services, and the Gelephu Mindfulness City.
3. Grow the private sector and attract foreign direct investment through better business regulations.
4. Improve productivity and competitiveness through skills, technology and innovation.
5. Strengthen domestic revenue (tax reform, GST) and prudent debt management.
6. Invest in human capital - quality education, health and skills that match the market.
7. Access climate finance and green investment based on Bhutan''s carbon-negative status.
These align with the 13th FYP goal of a high-income GNH economy by 2034.', null, '[{"point": "Meaning: met UN criteria (income, human assets index, economic vulnerability) - recognition of development progress; graduated 13 December 2023 (first to graduate with...); loss of LDC-specific benefits - concessional loans/ grants, trade preferences (duty-free access), aid for LDCs, support funds", "marks": 6}, {"point": "Effect on Developed Bhutan vision: positive confidence and investor image; challenges of reduced concessional finance, higher borrowing costs, competition, vulnerability remains (economic vulnerability index high)", "marks": 6}, {"point": "Strategies: smooth transition strategy, diversify economy and exports, private sector and FDI, improve competitiveness and productivity, domestic revenue mobilisation, negotiate continued preferences (e.g. GSP+), human capital and skills, digital economy, GMC, prudent debt, climate finance", "marks": 8}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'ldc-graduation-and-development'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 20,
    2160, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
