  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'Bhutan has seen an unprecedented number of Bhutanese migrating to more developed countries in pursuit of a better future. Among them, a large number are public and private sector employees resigning from their jobs.
How does this affect public service delivery and the economy at large? What can Bhutan do to ensure that critical services are delivered efficiently and that Bhutan remains a competitive nation? (20 marks)', 'Impact on public service delivery: many of those leaving are skilled professionals - nurses, doctors, teachers, engineers and ICT officers. Hospitals and schools face shortages, so patients wait longer and students lose teachers; offices lose experienced staff and institutional memory; the remaining staff are overloaded, which causes burnout and further resignations; and the quality and speed of services fall. Civil service attrition reached record levels in 2022-23.
Impact on the economy: Bhutan loses skilled workers and the public investment made in their education and training; businesses struggle to find qualified staff; productivity and tax revenue fall; the working-age population shrinks while the population ages; although remittances provide some foreign exchange, they do not replace lost skills.
What Bhutan can do:
1. Retain talent - revise pay and allowances (as done in 2023), offer clear career progression, recognition, professional development and better working conditions, especially for critical professions.
2. Fill gaps quickly - fast-track recruitment, train more nurses, teachers and doctors, re-employ retired and contract staff, recruit foreign professionals where necessary, and shift tasks to support staff.
3. Use technology - digitalise services and processes to do more with fewer people.
4. Create opportunities at home - grow the private sector, the Gelephu Mindfulness City and new industries so young people see a future in Bhutan.
5. Encourage return - reintegration programmes, recognition of foreign experience and investment opportunities for returnees.
6. Engage the diaspora - in knowledge sharing, investment and skills transfer.
7. Strengthen human resource planning to anticipate shortages.
These steps will help keep critical services running and keep Bhutan competitive.', null, '[{"point": "Impact on public services: shortages of nurses, doctors, teachers, engineers, IT staff; loss of experienced staff and institutional memory; overburdened remaining staff; delays and lower quality; high attrition (civil service 2022-23)", "marks": 7}, {"point": "Impact on economy: loss of skilled labour and productivity, wasted investment in education, labour shortages for businesses, ageing workforce, lower tax base; but remittances", "marks": 6}, {"point": "Measures: retention (pay revision, allowances, career progression, recognition), improved work culture, faster recruitment and training, contract and retired staff, task-shifting, technology and digitalisation, reintegration programme for returnees, engaging diaspora, economic opportunities (GMC, private sector), human resource planning", "marks": 7}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'migration-and-public-service'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 20,
    2160, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (General): BCSE 2023, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2023 Main Examination, General category, Paper III", "year": 2023, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, null, null, null, null, null, null)
